degree elevation is a good complementary route, with one important distinction.
A quadratic Bézier is exactly a parabola. Elevating it to a cubic preserves the same curve:
R0=Q0
R1=(Q0+2Q1)/3
R2=(2Q1+Q2)/3
R3=Q2
That cubic has no genuine cubic term; its third difference is zero. If the quadratic controls are integers, the elevated controls may need a factor of 3 to stay integer.
The integer-grid advantage is real: a point on a quadratic at t=p/q has denominators dividing q², versus q³ for a cubic. That is cheaper before the elevation factor of 3.
For an S, one parabola is not enough because its curvature has only one sign. Two quadratics can make a piecewise-parabolic S, one on each side of the inflection. If you start with two quadratics and elevate them, that piecewise-parabolic S is exact. If you start with the existing integer cubic S and split it at 1/2, each half is still a cubic; replacing each half by a quadratic is a fit, not an exact elevation.
The trade-off is fewer shape controls. Two parabolas give clean G1 joins and a simple S, but G2 still requires a curvature match, and there is less freedom than the asymmetric cubic. Parabola offsets are also not rational in general.
